# Isolated v2 post-issuance current reader SQL

Base commit: `ead43b78213175151c89e4e0bb0524791976d1c9`. Only new owned repository file: `docs/pilot/conversation/sql-drafts/p5-voice-v2-current.sql`. Read AGENTS, current continuation marker, lifetime-change contract, completed parent/preissue/proof implementations and unchanged legacy readers before implementing. Root owns aggregate evidence, commit and handoffs.

The approved endpoint is `public.bfa_p5_v2_read_current(p_request jsonb)`. Exact request keys are `protocol:2`, `ownerId`, `authSessionId`, `parentVersion`, `threadId`, `attemptId`, `authorityFingerprint`, `verifiedAt`, `identityExpiresAt`. Invalid shape/type/range raises a generic unavailable exception. Current ineligibility returns SQL NULL. Success returns exactly `{protocol:2,request:<entire original query>,current:<content-free snapshot>}`. The snapshot retains the legacy reader's owned-thread, allowlist, authority and approval fields, adds authority `issuedAt`, and is contained in a versioned envelope incompatible with the unchanged old boundary.

The common owner-first advisory lock precedes retained legacy-fence exclusion, exact current nonrevoked Auth parent/version, staging allowlist, thread lifecycle metadata, permanent generation, immutable issuance and protocol-2 parent binding, and unique exact-attempt approval. All associations must match the current owner/thread/incarnation/revision before the only permitted persistence effect. The reader never requires a claim, cleanup, receipt or capacity allocation, and never creates, adopts, retires, consumes or resets one. Thread reads select only lifecycle fields, not title, saved snapshot, messages or private content.

Clock decision approved by root and independently reviewed: advance only the shared owner high-water after every potentially waiting record lock. The new function locks `owner_clocks`, samples the existing `ms()` once, explicitly rejects NULL, unsafe or rollback time without writing, then retains safe monotonic time. This mirrors the established v2 pre-issuance clock effect while avoiding the unchanged `observe_now()` helper's artificial NULL-clock insert exception. No old helper was edited. A safe observed deadline expiry cannot later become current through a clock rollback; clock retention alone does not create other lifecycle terminality. All later proof/admission/marker transactions still independently recheck current state. Unexpected SQL exceptions can roll back the clock update; a missing reply does not establish rollback.

Original verification age remains strictly below 30000 ms, verified time cannot be in the future, and current Auth/allowlist/issuance/approval must each be unexpired. Issuance retains its original safe issued/expiry values and positive lifetime at most 900000 ms. Issued time is compared with actual observed database time, not the earlier Auth verification sample. The original issuance expiry is intentionally not required to be less than the *current* Auth expiry: fresh Auth may impose a shorter ceiling, which a future verifier must conservatively combine without rewriting either record. No original deadline is refreshed or shortened in this reader.

Initial pre-execution source review caught SQL identifier collisions between local request variables and visible column names. These were renamed to distinct `req_*` locals before any SQL test. Root then spotted a mechanical rename changing the JSON authority key to `req_fingerprint`; that literal was restored to approved `fingerprint` before tests. The independent reviewer re-read the corrected complete candidate and found no remaining source blocker. No previous source, schema, constraint, settings or runtime behavior changed.

Actual SQL tests are owned by the separate test agent and aggregate verification by root. Their later outcomes belong in the final evidence, not inferred from source review. This new draft is outside automatic migrations and has not been applied to connected SQL. Exact echoed input and service-role access do not establish real Auth ownership, protected service provenance, freshness after transport waits or permission to dispatch. Native concurrency, deployed Auth/provider/device acceptance, versioned verifier/owned orchestration and quiesced no-fallback cutover remain unfinished. No paid Lovable edit, real Auth/model/voice/microphone call, activation, publication or AWS/billing action occurred.

